would read Dan Pallotta's work.

00:16:04.483 --> 00:16:07.239
- And Dan's main book?
- Is "Uncharitable."

00:16:07.239 --> 00:16:09.898
So there's two things, you get
the numbers of how a charity

00:16:09.898 --> 00:16:11.062
spends it's money--

00:16:11.062 --> 00:16:12.912
- And you can create more
cynicism by reading the book.

00:16:12.912 --> 00:16:15.780
- You get a different view
reading the book saying that

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:18.250
overhead is not bad and
that we made overhead back.

00:16:18.250 --> 00:16:20.200
We have overhead
110 people cover it.

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:21.113
- Got it.

00:16:21.113 --> 00:16:23.077
- Then looking at how 
much of your money

00:16:23.077 --> 00:16:25.148
what we push for 
is transparency.

00:16:25.148 --> 00:16:29.177
I'm happy to give to a charity
where $.25 of my dollar might go

00:16:29.177 --> 00:16:31.059
to a smart team
running good programs.

00:16:31.059 --> 00:16:32.663
I don't want 50% of my dollar.

00:16:32.663 --> 00:16:33.885
I don't want my
90% of my dollar.

00:16:33.885 --> 00:16:35.235
- Let me ask you this.
- But I want to know.

00:16:35.235 --> 00:16:36.460
- Let's take this tact
since it's slippery slope.

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:37.958
You know me
very, very well.

00:16:37.958 --> 00:16:41.778
If I said I'm the marketing
genius of a generation but I

00:16:41.778 --> 00:16:43.736
need the other 17, I need DRock

00:16:43.736 --> 00:16:45.174
and he's fancy now he
makes movies.

00:16:45.174 --> 00:16:48.909
I need $.50 but I'm going to
kill it you think you can wrap

00:16:48.909 --> 00:16:50.685
your head around that?

00:16:50.685 --> 00:16:52.614
In theory you could, right?

00:16:52.614 --> 00:16:55.463
- Dan Pallotta would 
and that is one camp

00:16:55.463 --> 00:16:57.293
that says 50% is fine.

00:16:57.293 --> 00:16:59.617
- You're so close to it but that
feels so aggressive but at some

00:16:59.617 --> 00:17:04.827
level I guess the energy of it
could be, the punch line is

00:17:04.827 --> 00:17:06.261
if you can feel 
that the overhead

00:17:06.261 --> 00:17:08.680
actually justifies 
the mission at hand--

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:09.764
- But that's it.

00:17:09.764 --> 00:17:11.494
So the transparency is
what we are pushing for.

00:17:11.494 --> 00:17:14.192
So you might be willing to 
write $100,000 check and

00:17:14.192 --> 00:17:15.699
have 50 grand go.
- Yes.

00:17:15.699 --> 00:17:17.793
- The problem is some many people
don't know how money's handled.

00:17:17.793 --> 00:17:18.425
- That's right.

00:17:18.425 --> 00:17:20.745
- But I may not be or maybe
you and I are both willing.

00:17:20.745 --> 00:17:23.072
Maybe India you're
like $.50 is too much.

00:17:23.942 --> 00:17:25.924
That's the only thing that
we have been pushing for.

00:17:25.924 --> 00:17:27.856
I'm not telling people
to adopt the 100% model.

00:17:27.856 --> 00:17:30.365
It works for us--
- Because you're able--

00:17:30.365 --> 00:17:32.025
- the problem I was 
trying to solve.

00:17:32.025 --> 00:17:33.502
- Well members that
have covered your raise.

00:17:33.502 --> 00:17:34.284
- That's right.

00:17:34.284 --> 00:17:35.644
And people gived 
for the first time.

00:17:35.644 --> 00:17:36.517
I hear it all the time this is

00:17:36.517 --> 00:17:38.616
the first charitable gift
I've ever made my life.

00:17:38.616 --> 00:17:40.571
I just heard it last
week someone on Twitter.

00:17:40.571 --> 00:17:42.579
Made the first charitable gift
of my life, A, that's a little

00:17:42.579 --> 00:17:46.596
sad but that's the kind of
person I want who doesn't trust.

00:17:46.596 --> 00:17:47.779
- I would argue it's not sad.

00:17:47.779 --> 00:17:49.390
I think back to that 42%.

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:54.178
I was a grown man with a lot of
thoughts and a very decent dude

00:17:54.178 --> 00:17:58.391
