00:19:06.278 --> 00:19:08.714
- I do but jobs are
incredibly important.

00:19:08.714 --> 00:19:10.431
Shelter is important.

00:19:10.431 --> 00:19:12.322
Food is important.

00:19:12.322 --> 00:19:13.174
Health is important.

00:19:13.174 --> 00:19:15.966
We've just started with water
because I get to touch jobs.

00:19:15.966 --> 00:19:18.708
We hear these amazing stories of
women who will use the time back

00:19:18.708 --> 00:19:21.853
in their day specifically Ashley
in Uganda sometimes and they

00:19:21.853 --> 00:19:24.561
will sell rice at the market, 
they'll sell peanuts.

00:19:24.561 --> 00:19:25.627
I was in Zambia--

00:19:25.627 --> 00:19:28.424
- By the way, we're going very
quickly here, it's how we roll.

00:19:28.424 --> 00:19:30.012
That's how this show rolls.

00:19:30.012 --> 00:19:32.979
I know my audience 
guys when he says,

00:19:32.979 --> 00:19:34.879
gals when he says time

00:19:34.879 --> 00:19:36.885
back these are women

00:19:36.885 --> 00:19:40.675
who would walk three 
hours because an hour

00:19:40.675 --> 00:19:44.709
there, 30 minutes, 20 minutes
to scoop up the crap water.

00:19:44.709 --> 00:19:46.096
Brown.

00:19:46.096 --> 00:19:46.917
Brown.

00:19:46.917 --> 00:19:49.562
And then walk back.

00:19:49.562 --> 00:19:53.266
- 40 billion hours are wasted
just in Africa collecting water.

00:19:53.266 --> 00:19:54.634
We need to talk the workforce.

00:19:56.284 --> 00:19:59.291
They did a study, 88-page study
out of the UN, every $1 invested

00:19:59.291 --> 00:20:02.776
